  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 AM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nThe main weather concern with this forecast issuance continues to be \nfocused on precipitation type and amounts today through Thursday \nnight (Thanksgiving). There is better agreement of the latest \nguidance with respect to evolution of a deepening northern wave of \nenergy/closed low as it dives southeastward across MO. The colder \nair associated with that low will overlap with deeper moisture \nflowing in from the Gulf, leading to a mix of rain and snow, likely \nbecoming all snow across portions of southeast MO and counties \nbordering the Mississippi River in southern IL nearest to the upper \nlow. At least that is how it looks right now, but the rain/snow line \nmay still shift farther to the west. If that ends up happening, \nheadlines would likely need to be pulled farther west with later \nupdates. It is still worth keeping a close eye on as this is \nanything but a slam dunk weather forecast.\n\nUpper-level jet energy will help to enhance/generate 850-700 FGEN \nbanding, which pivots/focuses along and just west of the Mississippi \nRiver. On the colder side of the FGEN (SEMO) heavier snowfall rates \ncan be expected, likely exceeding the melting rate of the warmer \nroad/ground surfaces. That will lead to slippery road conditions \ndeveloping later this afternoon and especially tonight into Thursday \nmorning. As previously noted, ,there will be a very sharp \ngradient along the rain/snow line, which still makes the \nforecast fairly challenging. Locations east of the Mississippi \nRiver will see mainly rain through this event with rainfall \ntotals approaching or exceeding 1-2 inches.\n\nReviewing the probabilistic data from multiple ensemble sources, \nshows that locations west of the MIssissippi River, where the \nWarning is in place, has a 60%-90% chance of seeing more than 4 \ninches of snow tonight into Thursday morning. In fact, it won't be \ntoo surprising if we see a few totals reaching or exceeding 6 inches \non grassy/elevated surfaces. Totals that high will result in travel \nimpacts from slushy/snow covered roads tonight. Those numbers drop \noff quickly for the first tier of counties east of the Mississippi \nRiver ranging from 30% to 60% with the best chances being right \nalong the river. This will likely be the quick transition zone from \nrain to snow.  At this point decided to transition the Winter Storm \nWatch to a Winter Weather Advisory for those counties due to the \nlower totals and more limited impacts. It still may end up that \nthose counties in southern IL may end up seeing mainly rain.\nWhere snow occurs it will be very heavy/wet snow as soundings\nare fairly warm, with the moisture hugging the freezing line\nthrough a fairly deep layer and in fact crosses it in a few\nnarrow layers.\n\nThe next round of precipitation in the form of showers and maybe \nthunderstorms will arrive toward the end of the weekend into early \nnext week. Still a lot to iron out with that and plenty of \nuncertainty, but it does look warm enough to keep the winter weather \naway for that time period.",
